{"id":"ltc-related-payments-sf","title":"San Francisco nursing homes: what they paid related companies, 2019 to 2025","question":"Which related companies did each San Francisco nursing home pay, for what, and how much each year?","finding":"San Francisco's 13 nursing homes with a 2025 State report listed $19.0 million of transactions with related parties in 2025, 9.5% of their expenses (California: 8.6%). By year: 2019 $12.6 million, 2022 $20.7 million, 2023 $13.6 million, 2024 $13.0 million, 2025 $19.0 million. The largest single line in 2025: The Sequoias San Francisco to Sequoia Living, Inc (management, home office and administration), $3.4 million. Rent to related landlords came to $3.5 million and management or home-office charges to $12.8 million in 2025.","how":"Every transaction San Francisco skilled-nursing homes listed on page 10.4 of their State report (2020 form, lines 30 to 99: name of the related party, goods or services received, cost centre, transaction amount, and for Medi-Cal providers the adjustment) and, for 2019, the related-party costs listed on page 3.1 (lines 10 to 34) of the older form. Amounts are the transaction amounts as reported; negative entries (a landlord's depreciation or interest backed out) are left out of the sums. The kind of payment is read from the cost centre the home named and the description it gave. Where the report names a person rather than a company, the person is not shown.","limits":"Doing business with a related company is permitted and must be disclosed; these are the homes' own disclosures. Medi-Cal's rules limit what a home may claim for a related-party purchase to the related company's own cost, and the adjustment column shows homes doing that themselves. A chain that charges a management fee usually also provides services a home would otherwise buy. 2023 to 2025 reports are unaudited. Years 2020 and 2021 are not in the files supplied.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k"